      "content": "When migrating a multi-block bash script's own inline `case \"$VAR\" in ... esac` procedure out into a standalone script that itself contains several structurally-similar case statements for different purposes (argument validation, mode selection, and the actual migrated logic), any external lint/grep that used to extract 'the' case block via a bare `/case pattern/,/^esac/` awk range will silently break or capture the wrong block once there is more than one such statement in the new file -- especially since a case nested inside an `if` block closes with an indented `esac`, which a `/^esac/` (column-0-anchored) end pattern will never match, causing the range to run to EOF instead of stopping where intended. Grep the whole codebase for external scripts (lints, tests) that structurally parse the code being moved BEFORE finishing a migration, and re-anchor them on a more specific, nearby textual marker (e.g. the gating `if` condition) rather than assuming the moved block is still the file's only occurrence of its opening pattern.",
